## Solution Overview
Use closure to encapsulate rate limiter state with request queue and timer-based enforcement.

## Key Components
- Queue array for pending requests
- Processing flag to prevent race conditions
- Promise chaining for FIFO ordering
- Timer-based rate limit enforcement

## Implementation
```javascript
const createRateLimiter = (limit, interval) => {
  const queue = [];
  let processing = false;
  let lastRequest = 0;

  return async (request) => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      queue.push({ request, resolve, reject });
      processQueue();
    });
  };

  async function processQueue() {
    if (processing || queue.length === 0) return;
    processing = true;

    const now = Date.now();
    const timeSinceLastRequest = now - lastRequest;
    const delay = timeSinceLastRequest < interval ? interval - timeSinceLastRequest : 0;

    setTimeout(async () => {
      const { request, resolve, reject } = queue.shift();
      lastRequest = Date.now();

      try {
        const result = await request();
        resolve(result);
      } catch (error) {
        reject(error);
      }

      processing = false;
      processQueue();
    }, delay);
  }
};
```

## Benefits
- Maintains request order through FIFO queue
- Prevents rate limit violations with timing control
- Handles concurrent requests gracefully

## Concept Overview
Dynamic programming optimizes recursive problems by caching subproblem results. In React, useMemo prevents expensive recalculations during re-renders.

## Implementation Details
### Top-Down Memoization (Recursive)
```javascript
const fibonacci = useMemo(() => {
  const memo = {};
  const fib = (n) => {
    if (n in memo) return memo[n];
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    memo[n] = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
    return memo[n];
  };
  return fib;
}, []);
```

### Bottom-Up Tabulation (Iterative)
```javascript
const fibonacci = useMemo(() => {
  return (n) => {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    let prev = 0, curr = 1;
    for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
      [prev, curr] = [curr, prev + curr];
    }
    return curr;
  };
}, []);
```

## Common Pitfalls
- **Dependency array**: Empty array [] ensures function isn't recreated
- **Memory leaks**: Large memo objects can cause memory issues
- **Over-optimization**: useMemo overhead may exceed benefits for simple calculations
- **Referential equality**: useMemo preserves function reference for child components

